<body>
<p align=3D"CENTER">
<b>BERKSHIRE HATHAWAY INC.</b>
</p>

<pre>

<b>To the Shareholders of Berkshire Hathaway Inc.:</b>
=20
     Our gain in net worth during 1987 was $464 million, or=20
19.5%.  Over the last 23 years (that is, since present management=20
took over), our per-share book value has grown from $19.46 to=20
$2,477.47, or at a rate of 23.1% compounded annually.=20

     What counts, of course, is the rate of gain in per-share=20
business value, not book value.  In many cases, a corporation's=20
book value and business value are almost totally unrelated.  For=20
example, just before they went bankrupt, LTV and Baldwin-United=20
published yearend audits showing their book values to be $652=20
million and $397 million, respectively.  Conversely, Belridge Oil=20
was sold to Shell in 1979 for $3.6 billion although its book=20
value was only $177 million.=20

     At Berkshire, however, the two valuations have tracked=20
rather closely, with the growth rate in business value over the=20
last decade moderately outpacing the growth rate in book value. =20
This good news continued in 1987.=20

     Our premium of business value to book value has widened for=20
two simple reasons: We own some remarkable businesses and they=20
are run by even more remarkable managers.=20

     You have a right to question that second assertion.  After=20
all, CEOs seldom tell their shareholders that they have assembled=20
a bunch of turkeys to run things.  Their reluctance to do so=20
makes for some strange annual reports.  Oftentimes, in his=20
shareholders' letter, a CEO will go on for pages detailing=20
corporate performance that is woefully inadequate.  He will=20
nonetheless end with a warm paragraph describing his managerial=20
comrades as "our most precious asset." Such comments sometimes=20
make you wonder what the other assets can possibly be.=20

     At Berkshire, however, my appraisal of our operating=20
managers is, if anything, understated.  To understand why, first=20
take a look at page 7, where we show the earnings (on an=20
historical-cost accounting basis) of our seven largest non-
financial units:  Buffalo News, Fechheimer, Kirby, Nebraska=20
Furniture Mart, Scott Fetzer Manufacturing Group, See's Candies,=20
and World Book.  In 1987, these seven business units had combined=20
operating earnings before interest and taxes of $180 million.=20

     By itself, this figure says nothing about economic=20
performance.  To evaluate that, we must know how much total=20
capital - debt and equity - was needed to produce these earnings. =20
Debt plays an insignificant role at our seven units: Their net=20
interest expense in 1987 was only $2 million.  Thus, pre-tax=20
earnings on the equity capital employed by these businesses=20
amounted to $178 million.  And this equity - again on an=20
historical-cost basis - was only $175 million.=20

     If these seven business units had operated as a single=20
company, their 1987 <i>after</i>-tax earnings would have been=20
approximately $100 million - a return of about 57% on equity=20
capital.  You'll seldom see such a percentage anywhere, let alone=20
at large, diversified companies with nominal leverage.  Here's a=20
benchmark: In its 1988 Investor's Guide issue, <u>Fortune</u> reported=20
that among the 500 largest industrial companies and 500 largest=20
service companies, only six had averaged a return on equity of=20
over 30% during the previous decade.  The best performer among=20
the 1000 was Commerce Clearing House at 40.2%.=20
=20
     Of course, the returns that Berkshire earns from these seven=20
units are not as high as their underlying returns because, in=20
aggregate, we bought the businesses at a substantial premium to=20
underlying equity capital.  Overall, these operations are carried=20
on our books at about $222 million above the historical=20
accounting values of the underlying assets.  However, the=20
managers of the units should be judged by the returns they=20
achieve on the underlying assets; what we pay for a business does=20
not affect the amount of capital its manager has to work with.=20
(If, to become a shareholder and part owner of Commerce Clearing=20
House, you pay, say, six times book value, that does not change=20
CCH's return on equity.)=20

     Three important inferences can be drawn from the figures I=20
have cited.  First, the current business value of these seven=20
units is far above their historical book value and also far above=20
the value at which they are carried on Berkshire's balance sheet. =20
Second, because so little capital is required to run these=20
businesses, they can grow while concurrently making almost all of=20
their earnings available for deployment in new opportunities. =20
Third, these businesses are run by truly extraordinary managers. =20
The Blumkins, the Heldmans, Chuck Huggins, Stan Lipsey, and Ralph=20
Schey all meld unusual talent, energy and character to achieve=20
exceptional financial results.=20

     For good reasons, we had very high expectations when we=20
joined with these managers.  In every case, however, our=20
experience has greatly exceeded those expectations.  We have=20
received far more than we deserve, but we are willing to accept=20
such inequities. (We subscribe to the view Jack Benny expressed=20
upon receiving an acting award: "I don't deserve this, but then,=20
I have arthritis and I don't deserve that either.")=20

     Beyond the Sainted Seven, we have our other major unit,=20
insurance, which I believe also has a business value well above=20
the net assets employed in it.  However, appraising the business=20
value of a property-casualty insurance company is a decidedly=20
imprecise process.  The industry is volatile, reported earnings=20
oftentimes are seriously inaccurate, and recent changes in the=20
Tax Code will severely hurt future profitability.  Despite these=20
problems, we like the business and it will almost certainly=20
remain our largest operation.  Under Mike Goldberg's management,=20
the insurance business should treat us well over time.=20

     With managers like ours, my partner, Charlie Munger, and I=20
have little to do with operations. in fact, it is probably fair=20
to say that if we did more, less would be accomplished.  We have=20
no corporate meetings, no corporate budgets, and no performance=20
reviews (though our managers, of course, oftentimes find such=20
procedures useful at their operating units).  After all, what can=20
we tell the Blumkins about home furnishings, or the Heldmans=20
about uniforms?=20

     Our major contribution to the operations of our subsidiaries=20
is applause.  But it is not the indiscriminate applause of a=20
Pollyanna.  Rather it is informed applause based upon the two=20
long careers we have spent intensively observing business=20
performance and managerial behavior.  Charlie and I have seen so=20
much of the ordinary in business that we can truly appreciate a=20
virtuoso performance.  Only one response to the 1987 performance=20
of our operating managers is appropriate: sustained, deafening=20
applause.=20

