     Gypsy Rose Lee announced on one of her later birthdays: "I=20
have everything I had last year; it's just that it's all two=20
inches lower." As the table shows, during 1987 almost all of our=20
businesses aged in a more upbeat way.=20

     There's not a lot new to report about these businesses - and=20
that's good, not bad.  Severe change and exceptional returns=20
usually don't mix.  Most investors, of course, behave as if just=20
the opposite were true.  That is, they usually confer the highest=20
price-earnings ratios on exotic-sounding businesses that hold out=20
the promise of feverish change.  That prospect lets investors=20
fantasize about future profitability rather than face today's=20
business realities.  For such investor-dreamers, any blind date=20
is preferable to one with the girl next door, no matter how=20
desirable she may be.=20

     Experience, however, indicates that the best business=20
returns are usually achieved by companies that are doing=20
something quite similar today to what they were doing five or ten=20
years ago.  That is no argument for managerial complacency. =20
Businesses always have opportunities to improve service, product=20
lines, manufacturing techniques, and the like, and obviously=20
these opportunities should be seized.  But a business that=20
constantly encounters major change also encounters many chances=20
for major error.  Furthermore, economic terrain that is forever=20
shifting violently is ground on which it is difficult to build a=20
fortress-like business franchise.  Such a franchise is usually=20
the key to sustained high returns.=20

     The <u>Fortune</u> study I mentioned earlier supports our view. =20
Only 25 of the 1,000 companies met two tests of economic=20
excellence - an average return on equity of over 20% in the ten=20
years, 1977 through 1986, and no year worse than 15%.  These=20
business superstars were also stock market superstars: During the=20
decade, 24 of the 25 outperformed the S&amp;P 500.=20
=20
     The <u>Fortune</u> champs may surprise you in two respects.  First,=20
most use very little leverage compared to their interest-paying=20
capacity.  Really good businesses usually don't need to borrow. =20
Second, except for one company that is "high-tech" and several=20
others that manufacture ethical drugs, the companies are in=20
businesses that, on balance, seem rather mundane.  Most sell non-
sexy products or services in much the same manner as they did ten=20
years ago (though in larger quantities now, or at higher prices,=20
or both).  The record of these 25 companies confirms that making=20
the most of an already strong business franchise, or=20
concentrating on a single winning business theme, is what usually=20
produces exceptional economics.=20

     Berkshire's experience has been similar.  Our managers have=20
produced extraordinary results by doing rather ordinary things -=20
but doing them exceptionally well.  Our managers protect their=20
franchises, they control costs, they search for new products and=20
markets that build on their existing strengths and they don't get=20
diverted.  They work exceptionally hard at the details of their=20
businesses, and it shows.=20

     Here's an update:=20

   <b>o</b> Agatha Christie, whose husband was an archaeologist, said=20
that was the perfect profession for one's spouse: "The older you=20
become, the more interested they are in you." It is students of=20
business management, not archaeologists, who should be interested=20
in Mrs. B (Rose Blumkin), the 94-year-old chairman of Nebraska=20
Furniture Mart.=20

     Fifty years ago Mrs. B started the business with $500, and=20
today NFM is far and away the largest home furnishings store in=20
the country.  Mrs. B continues to work seven days a week at the=20
job from the opening of each business day until the close.  She=20
buys, she sells, she manages - and she runs rings around the=20
competition.  It's clear to me that she's gathering speed and may=20
well reach her full potential in another five or ten years. =20
Therefore, I've persuaded the Board to scrap our mandatory=20
retirement-at-100 policy. (And it's about time:  With every=20
passing year, this policy has seemed sillier to me.)=20

     Net sales of NFM were $142.6 million in 1987, up 8% from=20
1986.  There's nothing like this store in the country, and=20
there's nothing like the family Mrs. B has produced to carry on:=20
Her son Louie, and his three boys, Ron, Irv and Steve, possess=20
the business instincts, integrity and drive of Mrs. B. They work=20
as a team and, strong as each is individually, the whole is far=20
greater than the sum of the parts.=20

     The superb job done by the Blumkins benefits us as owners,=20
but even more dramatically benefits NFM's customers.  They saved=20
about $30 million in 1987 by buying from NFM.  In other words,=20
the goods they bought would have cost that much more if purchased=20
elsewhere.=20

     You'll enjoy an anonymous letter I received last August:=20
"Sorry to see Berkshire profits fall in the second quarter.  One=20
way you may gain back part of your lost. (sic) Check the pricing=20
at The Furniture Mart.  You will find that they are leaving 10%=20
to 20% on the table.  This additional profit on $140 million of=20
sells (sic) is $28 million.  Not small change in anyone's pocket! =20
Check out other furniture, carpet, appliance and T.V. dealers. =20
Your raising prices to a reasonable profit will help.  Thank you.=20
/signed/ A Competitor."=20

     NFM will continue to grow and prosper by following Mrs. B's=20
maxim:  "Sell cheap and tell the truth."=20

   <b>o</b> Among dominant papers of its size or larger, the Buffalo=20
News continues to be the national leader in two important ways:=20
(1) its weekday and Sunday penetration rate (the percentage of=20
households in the paper's primary market area that purchase it);=20
and (2) its "news-hole" percentage (the portion of the paper=20
devoted to news).

     It may not be coincidence that one newspaper leads in both=20
categories: an exceptionally "newsrich" product makes for broad=20
audience appeal, which in turn leads to high penetration.  Of=20
course, quantity must be matched by quality.  This not only=20
means good reporting and good writing; it means freshness and=20
relevance.  To be indispensable, a paper must promptly tell its=20
readers many things they want to know but won't otherwise learn=20
until much later, if ever.=20
=20
     At the News, we put out seven fresh editions every 24 hours,=20
each one extensively changed in content.  Here's a small example=20
that may surprise you: We redo the obituary page in every edition=20
of the News, or seven times a day.  Any obituary added runs=20
through the next six editions until the publishing cycle has been=20
completed.=20

     It's vital, of course, for a newspaper to cover national and=20
international news well and in depth.  But it is also vital for=20
it to do what only a local newspaper can: promptly and=20
extensively chronicle the personally-important, otherwise-
unreported details of community life.  Doing this job well=20
requires a very broad range of news - and that means lots of=20
space, intelligently used.=20

     Our news hole was about 50% in 1987, just as it has been=20
year after year.  If we were to cut it to a more typical 40%, we=20
would save approximately $4 million annually in newsprint costs. =20
That interests us not at all - and it won't interest us even if,=20
for one reason or another, our profit margins should=20
significantly shrink.=20

     Charlie and I do not believe in flexible operating budgets,=20
as in "Non-direct expenses can be X if revenues are Y, but must=20
be reduced if revenues are Y - 5%." Should we really cut our news=20
hole at the Buffalo News, or the quality of product and service=20
at See's, simply because profits are down during a given year or=20
quarter?  Or, conversely, should we add a staff economist, a=20
corporate strategist, an institutional advertising campaign or=20
something else that does Berkshire no good simply because the=20
money currently is rolling in?=20

     That makes no sense to us.  We neither understand the adding=20
of unneeded people or activities because profits are booming, nor=20
the cutting of essential people or activities because=20
profitability is shrinking.  That kind of yo-yo approach is=20
neither business-like nor humane.  Our goal is to do what makes=20
sense for Berkshire's customers and employees at all times, and=20
never to add the unneeded. ("But what about <font size=3D"-1">the corporate=
 jet</font>?"=20
you rudely ask.  Well, occasionally a man must rise above=20
principle.)=20

     Although the News' revenues have grown only moderately since=20
1984, superb management by Stan Lipsey, its publisher, has=20
produced excellent profit growth.  For several years, I have=20
incorrectly predicted that profit margins at the News would fall. =20
This year I will not let vou down: Margins will, without=20
question, shrink in 1988 and profit may fall as well. =20
Skyrocketing newsprint costs will be the major cause.=20

   <b>o</b> Fechheimer Bros. Company is another of our family=20
businesses - and, like the Blumkins, what a family.  Three=20
generations of Heldmans have for decades consistently, built the=20
sales and profits of this manufacturer and distributor of=20
uniforms.  In the year that Berkshire acquired its controlling=20
interest in Fechheimer - 1986 - profits were a record.  The=20
Heldmans didn't slow down after that.  Last year earnings=20
increased substantially and the outlook is good for 1988.=20

     There's nothing magic about the Uniform business; the only=20
magic is in the Heldmans.  Bob, George, Gary, Roger and Fred know=20
the business inside and out, and they have fun running it.  We=20
are fortunate to be in partnership with them.=20

   <b>o</b> Chuck Huggins continues to set new records at See's, just as=20
he has ever since we put him in charge on the day of our purchase=20
some 16 years ago.  In 1987, volume hit a new high at slightly=20
Under 25 million pounds.  For the second year in a row, moreover,=20
same-store sales, measured in pounds, were virtually unchanged. =20
In case you are wondering, that represents improvement: In each=20
of the previous six years, same-store sales had fallen.=20

     Although we had a particularly strong 1986 Christmas season,=20
we racked up better store-for-store comparisons in the 1987=20
Christmas season than at any other time of the year.  Thus, the=20
seasonal factor at See's becomes even more extreme.  In 1987,=20
about 85% of our profit was earned during December.=20

     Candy stores are fun to visit, but most have not been fun=20
for their owners.  From what we can learn, practically no one=20
besides See's has made significant profits in recent years from=20
the operation of candy shops.  Clearly, Chuck's record at See's=20
is not due to a rising industry tide.  Rather, it is a one-of-a-
kind performance.=20
=20
     His achievement requires an excellent product - which we=20
have - but it also requires genuine affection for the customer. =20
Chuck is 100% customer-oriented, and his attitude sets the tone=20
for the rest of the See's organization.=20

     Here's an example of Chuck in action: At See's we regularly=20
add new pieces of candy to our mix and also cull a few to keep=20
our product line at about 100 varieties.  Last spring we selected=20
14 items for elimination.  Two, it turned out, were badly missed=20
by our customers, who wasted no time in letting us know what they=20
thought of our judgment: "A pox on all in See's who participated=20
in the abominable decision...;" "May your new truffles melt in=20
transit, may they sour in people's mouths, may your costs go up=20
and your profits go down...;" "We are investigating the=20
possibility of obtaining a mandatory injunction requiring you to=20
supply...;" You get the picture.  In all, we received many hundreds of=20
letters.=20

     Chuck not only reintroduced the pieces, he turned this=20
miscue into an opportunity.  Each person who had written got a=20
complete and honest explanation in return.  Said Chuck's letter:=20
"Fortunately, when I make poor decisions, good things often=20
happen as a result...;" And with the letter went a special gift=20
certificate.=20

     See's increased prices only slightly in the last two years. =20
In 1988 we have raised prices somewhat more, though still=20
moderately.  To date, sales have been weak and it may be=20
difficult for See's to improve its earnings this year.=20

   <b>o</b> World Book, Kirby, and the Scott Fetzer Manufacturing Group=20
are all under the management of Ralph Schey.  And what a lucky=20
thing for us that they are.  I told you last year that Scott=20
Fetzer performance in 1986 had far exceeded the expectations that=20
Charlie and I had at the time of our purchase.  Results in 1987=20
were even better.  Pre-tax earnings rose 10% while average=20
capital employed declined significantly.    =20

     Ralph's mastery of the 19 businesses for which he is=20
responsible is truly amazing, and he has also attracted some=20
outstanding managers to run them.  We would love to find a few=20
additional units that could be put under Ralph's wing.=20

     The businesses of Scott Fetzer are too numerous to describe=20
in detail.  Let's just update you on one of our favorites: At the=20
end of 1987, World Book introduced its most dramatically-revised=20
edition since 1962.  The number of color photos was increased=20
from 14,000 to 24,000; over 6,000 articles were revised; 840 new=20
contributors were added.  Charlie and I recommend this product to=20
you and your family, as we do World Book's products for younger=20
children, Childcraft and Early World of Learning.=20

     In 1987, World Book unit sales in the United States=20
increased for the fifth consecutive year.  International sales=20
and profits also grew substantially.  The outlook is good for=20
Scott Fetzer operations in aggregate, and for World Book in=20
particular.=20

