     The combined ratio represents total insurance costs (losses=20
incurred plus expenses) compared to revenue from premiums: A=20
ratio below 100 indicates an underwriting profit, and one above=20
100 indicates a loss.  When the investment income that an insurer=20
earns from holding on to policyholders' funds ("the float") is=20
taken into account, a combined ratio in the 107-111 range=20
typically produces an overall break-even result, exclusive of=20
earnings on the funds provided by shareholders.=20

     The math of the insurance business, encapsulated by the=20
table, is not very complicated.  In years when the industry's=20
annual gain in revenues (premiums) pokes along at 4% or 5%,=20
underwriting losses are sure to mount.  That is not because auto=20
accidents, fires, windstorms and the like are occurring more=20
frequently, nor has it lately been the fault of general=20
inflation.  Today, social and judicial inflation are the major=20
culprits; the cost of entering a courtroom has simply ballooned. =20
Part of the jump in cost arises from skyrocketing verdicts, and=20
part from the tendency of judges and juries to expand the=20
coverage of insurance policies beyond that contemplated by the=20
insurer when the policies were written.  Seeing no let-up in=20
either trend, we continue to believe that the industry's revenues=20
must grow at about 10% annually for it to just hold its own in=20
terms of profitability, even though general inflation may be=20
running at a considerably lower rate.=20

     The strong revenue gains of 1985-87 almost guaranteed the=20
industry an excellent underwriting performance in 1987 and,=20
indeed, it was a banner year.  But the news soured as the=20
quarters rolled by:  Best's estimates that year-over-year volume=20
increases were 12.9%, 11.1%, 5.7%, and 5.6%. In 1988, the
revenue gain is certain to be far below our 10% "equilibrium"=20
figure.  Clearly, the party is over.=20

     However, earnings will not immediately sink.  A lag factor=20
exists in this industry: Because most policies are written for a=20
one-year term, higher or lower insurance prices do not have their=20
full impact on earnings until many months after they go into=20
effect.  Thus, to resume our metaphor, when the party ends and=20
the bar is closed, you are allowed to finish your drink.  If=20
results are not hurt by a major natural catastrophe, we predict a=20
small climb for the industry's combined ratio in 1988, followed=20
by several years of larger increases.=20

     The insurance industry is cursed with a set of dismal=20
economic characteristics that make for a poor long-term outlook:=20
hundreds of competitors, ease of entry, and a product that cannot=20
be differentiated in any meaningful way.  In such a commodity-
like business, only a very low-cost operator or someone operating=20
in a protected, and usually small, niche can sustain high=20
profitability levels.=20

     When shortages exist, however, even commodity businesses=20
flourish.  The insurance industry enjoyed that kind of climate=20
for a while but it is now gone.  One of the ironies of capitalism=20
is that most managers in commodity industries abhor shortage=20
conditions - even though those are the only circumstances=20
permitting them good returns.  Whenever shortages appear, the=20
typical manager simply can't wait to expand capacity and thereby=20
plug the hole through which money is showering upon him.  This is=20
precisely what insurance managers did in 1985-87, confirming=20
again Disraeli's observation: "What we learn from history is that=20
we do not learn from history."=20

     At Berkshire, we work to escape the industry's commodity=20
economics in two ways. First, we differentiate our product by our=20
financial strength, which exceeds that of all others in the=20
industry.  This strength, however, is limited in its usefulness.=20
It means nothing in the personal insurance field:  The buyer of=20
an auto or homeowners policy is going to get his claim paid even=20
if his insurer fails (as many have).  It often means nothing in=20
the commercial insurance arena: When times are good, many major=20
corporate purchasers of insurance and their brokers pay scant=20
attention to the insurer's ability to perform under the more=20
adverse conditions that may exist, say, five years later when a=20
complicated claim is finally resolved. (Out of sight, out of mind=20
- and, later on, maybe out-of-pocket.)=20

     Periodically, however, buyers remember Ben Franklin's=20
observation that it is hard for an empty sack to stand upright=20
and recognize their need to buy promises only from insurers that=20
have enduring financial strength.  It is then that we have a=20
major competitive advantage.  When a buyer really focuses on=20
whether a $10 million claim can be easily paid by his insurer=20
five or ten years down the road, and when he takes into account=20
the possibility that poor underwriting conditions may then=20
coincide with depressed financial markets and defaults by=20
reinsurer, he will find only a few companies he can trust. =20
Among those, Berkshire will lead the pack.=20
=20
     Our second method of differentiating ourselves is the total=20
indifference to volume that we maintain.  In 1989, we will be=20
perfectly willing to write five times as much business as we=20
write in 1988 - or only one-fifth as much.  We hope, of course,=20
that conditions will allow us large volume.  But we cannot=20
control market prices.  If they are unsatisfactory, we will=20
simply do very little business.  No other major insurer acts with=20
equal restraint.=20

     Three conditions that prevail in insurance, but not in most=20
businesses, allow us our flexibility.  First, market share is not=20
an important determinant of profitability: In this business, in=20
contrast to the newspaper or grocery businesses, the economic=20
rule is not survival of the fattest.  Second, in many sectors of=20
insurance, including most of those in which we operate,=20
distribution channels are not proprietary and can be easily=20
entered: Small volume this year does not preclude huge volume=20
next year.  Third, idle capacity - which in this industry largely=20
means people - does not result in intolerable costs.  In a way=20
that industries such as printing or steel cannot, we can operate=20
at quarter-speed much of the time and still enjoy long-term=20
prosperity.=20

     We follow a price-based-on-exposure, not-on-competition=20
policy because it makes sense for our shareholders.  But we're=20
happy to report that it is also pro-social.  This policy means=20
that we are always available, given prices that we believe are=20
adequate, to write huge volumes of almost any type of property-
casualty insurance.  Many other insurers follow an in-and-out=20
approach.  When they are "out" - because of mounting losses,=20
capital inadequacy, or whatever - we are available.  Of course,=20
when others are panting to do business we are also available -=20
but at such times we often find ourselves priced above the=20
market.  In effect, we supply insurance buyers and brokers with a=20
large reservoir of standby capacity.=20

     One story from mid-1987 illustrates some consequences of our=20
pricing policy:  One of the largest family-owned insurance=20
brokers in the country is headed by a fellow who has long been a=20
shareholder of Berkshire.  This man handles a number of large=20
risks that are candidates for placement with our New York office. =20
Naturally, he does the best he can for his clients.  And, just as=20
naturally, when the insurance market softened dramatically in=20
1987 he found prices at other insurers lower than we were willing=20
to offer.  His reaction was, first, to place all of his business=20
elsewhere and, second, to buy more stock in Berkshire.  Had we=20
been really competitive, he said, we would have gotten his=20
insurance business but he would not have bought our stock.=20

     Berkshire's underwriting experience was excellent in 1987,=20
in part because of the lag factor discussed earlier.  Our=20
combined ratio (on a statutory basis and excluding structured=20
settlements and financial reinsurance) was 105.  Although the=20
ratio was somewhat less favorable than in 1986, when it was 103,=20
our profitability improved materially in 1987 because we had the=20
use of far more float.  This trend will continue to run in our=20
favor: Our ratio of float to premium volume will increase very=20
significantly during the next few years.  Thus, Berkshire's=20
insurance profits are quite likely to improve during 1988 and=20
1989, even though we expect our combined ratio to rise.=20

     Our insurance business has also made some important non-
financial gains during the last few years.  Mike Goldberg, its=20
manager, has assembled a group of talented professionals to write=20
larger risks and unusual coverages.  His operation is now well=20
equipped to handle the lines of business that will occasionally=20
offer us major opportunities.=20

     Our loss reserve development, detailed on pages 41-42, looks=20
better this year than it has previously.  But we write lots of=20
"long-tail" business - that is, policies generating claims that=20
often take many years to resolve.  Examples would be product=20
liability, or directors and officers liability coverages.  With a=20
business mix like this, one year of reserve development tells you=20
very little.=20

     You should be very suspicious of any earnings figures=20
reported by insurers (including our own, as we have unfortunately=20
proved to you in the past).  The record of the last decade shows=20
that a great many of our best-known insurers have reported=20
earnings to shareholders that later proved to be wildly=20
erroneous.  In most cases, these errors were totally innocent:=20
The unpredictability of our legal system makes it impossible for=20
even the most conscientious insurer to come close to judging the=20
eventual cost of long-tail claims.=20
=20
     Nevertheless, auditors annually certify the numbers given=20
them by management and in their opinions unqualifiedly state that=20
these figures "present fairly" the financial position of their=20
clients.  The auditors use this reassuring language even though=20
they know from long and painful experience that the numbers so=20
certified are likely to differ dramatically from the true=20
earnings of the period.  Despite this history of error, investors=20
understandably rely upon auditors' opinions.  After all, a=20
declaration saying that "the statements present fairly" hardly=20
sounds equivocal to the non-accountant.=20

     The wording in the auditor's standard opinion letter is=20
scheduled to change next year.  The new language represents=20
improvement, but falls far short of describing the limitations of=20
a casualty-insurer audit.  If it is to depict the true state of=20
affairs, we believe the standard opinion letter to shareholders=20
of a property-casualty company should read something like: "We=20
have relied upon representations of management in respect to the=20
liabilities shown for losses and loss adjustment expenses, the=20
estimate of which, in turn, very materially affects the earnings=20
and financial condition herein reported.  We can express no=20
opinion about the accuracy of these figures.  Subject to that=20
important reservation, in our opinion, etc."=20

     If lawsuits develop in respect to wildly inaccurate=20
financial statements (which they do), auditors will definitely=20
say something of that sort in court anyway.  Why should they not=20
be forthright about their role and its limitations from the=20
outset?=20

     We want to emphasize that we are not faulting auditors for=20
their inability to accurately assess loss reserves (and therefore=20
earnings).  We fault them only for failing to publicly=20
acknowledge that they can't do this job.=20

     From all appearances, the innocent mistakes that are=20
constantly made in reserving are accompanied by others that are=20
deliberate.  Various charlatans have enriched themselves at the=20
expense of the investing public by exploiting, first, the=20
inability of auditors to evaluate reserve figures and, second,=20
the auditors' willingness to confidently certify those figures as=20
if they had the expertise to do so.  We will continue to see such=20
chicanery in the future.  Where "earnings" can be created by the=20
stroke of a pen, the dishonest will gather.  For them, long-tail=20
insurance is heaven.  The audit wording we suggest would at least=20
serve to put investors on guard against these predators.=20

     The taxes that insurance companies pay - which increased=20
materially, though on a delayed basis, upon enactment of the Tax=20
Reform Act of 1986 - took a further turn for the worse at the end=20
of 1987.  We detailed the 1986 changes in last year's report.  We=20
also commented on the irony of a statute that substantially=20
increased 1987 reported earnings for insurers even as it=20
materially reduced both their long-term earnings potential and=20
their business value.  At Berkshire, the temporarily-helpful=20
"fresh start" adjustment inflated 1987 earnings by $8.2 million.=20

     In our opinion, the 1986 Act was the most important economic=20
event affecting the insurance industry over the past decade.  The=20
1987 Bill further reduced the intercorporate dividends-received=20
credit from 80% to 70%, effective January 1, 1988, except for=20
cases in which the taxpayer owns at least 20% of an investee.=20

     Investors who have owned stocks or bonds through corporate=20
intermediaries other than qualified investment companies have=20
always been disadvantaged in comparison to those owning the same=20
securities directly.  The penalty applying to indirect ownership=20
was greatly increased by the 1986 Tax Bill and, to a lesser=20
extent, by the 1987 Bill, particularly in instances where the=20
intermediary is an insurance company.  We have no way of=20
offsetting this increased level of taxation.  It simply means=20
that a given set of pre-tax investment returns will now translate=20
into much poorer after-tax results for our shareholders.=20

     All in all, we expect to do well in the insurance business,=20
though our record is sure to be uneven.  The immediate outlook is=20
for substantially lower volume but reasonable earnings=20
improvement.  The decline in premium volume will accelerate after=20
our quota-share agreement with Fireman's Fund expires in 1989. =20
At some point, likely to be at least a few years away, we may see=20
some major opportunities, for which we are now much better=20
prepared than we were in 1985.=20
=20
<b>Marketable Securities - Permanent Holdings</b>

     Whenever Charlie and I buy common stocks for Berkshire's=20
insurance companies (leaving aside arbitrage purchases, discussed=20
later) we approach the transaction as if we were buying into a=20
private business.  We look at the economic prospects of the=20
business, the people in charge of running it, and the price we=20
must pay.  We do not have in mind any time or price for sale. =20
Indeed, we are willing to hold a stock indefinitely so long as we=20
expect the business to increase in intrinsic value at a=20
satisfactory rate.  When investing, we view ourselves as business=20
analysts - not as market analysts, not as macroeconomic analysts,=20
and not even as security analysts.=20

     Our approach makes an active trading market useful, since it=20
periodically presents us with mouth-watering opportunities.  But=20
by no means is it essential: a prolonged suspension of trading in=20
the securities we hold would not bother us any more than does the=20
lack of daily quotations on World Book or Fechheimer. =20
Eventually, our economic fate will be determined by the economic=20
fate of the business we own, whether our ownership is partial or=20
total.=20

     Ben Graham, my friend and teacher, long ago described the=20
mental attitude toward market fluctuations that I believe to be=20
most conducive to investment success.  He said that you should=20
imagine market quotations as coming from a remarkably=20
accommodating fellow named Mr. Market who is your partner in a=20
private business.  Without fail, Mr. Market appears daily and=20
names a price at which he will either buy your interest or sell=20
you his.=20

     Even though the business that the two of you own may have=20
economic characteristics that are stable, Mr. Market's quotations=20
will be anything but.  For, sad to say, the poor fellow has=20
incurable emotional problems.  At times he feels euphoric and can=20
see only the favorable factors affecting the business.  When in=20
that mood, he names a very high buy-sell price because he fears=20
that you will snap up his interest and rob him of imminent gains. =20
At other times he is depressed and can see nothing but trouble=20
ahead for both the business and the world.  On these occasions he=20
will name a very low price, since he is terrified that you will=20
unload your interest on him.=20

     Mr. Market has another endearing characteristic: He doesn't=20
mind being ignored.  If his quotation is uninteresting to you=20
today, he will be back with a new one tomorrow.  Transactions are=20
strictly at your option.  Under these conditions, the more manic-
depressive his behavior, the better for you.=20

     But, like Cinderella at the ball, you must heed one warning=20
or everything will turn into pumpkins and mice: Mr. Market is=20
there to serve you, not to guide you.  It is his pocketbook, not=20
his wisdom, that you will find useful.  If he shows up some day=20
in a particularly foolish mood, you are free to either ignore him=20
or to take advantage of him, but it will be disastrous if you=20
fall under his influence.  Indeed, if you aren't certain that you=20
understand and can value your business far better than Mr.=20
Market, you don't belong in the game.  As they say in poker, "If=20
you've been in the game 30 minutes and you don't know who the=20
patsy is, <i>you're</i> the patsy."=20

     Ben's Mr. Market allegory may seem out-of-date in today's=20
investment world, in which most professionals and academicians=20
talk of efficient markets, dynamic hedging and betas.  Their=20
interest in such matters is understandable, since techniques=20
shrouded in mystery clearly have value to the purveyor of=20
investment advice.  After all, what witch doctor has ever=20
achieved fame and fortune by simply advising "Take two aspirins"?=20

     The value of market esoterica to the consumer of investment=20
advice is a different story.  In my opinion, investment success=20
will not be produced by arcane formulae, computer programs or=20
signals flashed by the price behavior of stocks and markets. =20
Rather an investor will succeed by coupling good business=20
judgment with an ability to insulate his thoughts and behavior=20
from the super-contagious emotions that swirl about the=20
marketplace.  In my own efforts to stay insulated, I have found=20
it highly useful to keep Ben's Mr. Market concept firmly in mind.=20
=20
     Following Ben's teachings, Charlie and I let our marketable=20
equities tell us by their operating results - not by their daily,=20
or even yearly, price quotations - whether our investments are=20
successful.  The market may ignore business success for a while,=20
but eventually will confirm it.  As Ben said: "In the short run,=20
the market is a voting machine but in the long run it is a=20
weighing machine." The speed at which a business's success is=20
recognized, furthermore, is not that important as long as the=20
company's intrinsic value is increasing at a satisfactory rate. =20
In fact, delayed recognition can be an advantage: It may give us=20
the chance to buy more of a good thing at a bargain price.=20

     Sometimes, of course, the market may judge a business to be=20
more valuable than the underlying facts would indicate it is.  In=20
such a case, we will sell our holdings.  Sometimes, also, we will=20
sell a security that is fairly valued or even undervalued because=20
we require funds for a still more undervalued investment or one=20
we believe we understand better.=20

     We need to emphasize, however, that we do not sell holdings=20
just because they have appreciated or because we have held them=20
for a long time. (Of Wall Street maxims the most foolish may be=20
"You can't go broke taking a profit.") We are quite content to=20
hold any security indefinitely, so long as the prospective return=20
on equity capital of the underlying business is satisfactory,=20
management is competent and honest, and the market does not=20
overvalue the business.=20

     However, our insurance companies own three marketable common=20
stocks that we would not sell even though they became far=20
overpriced in the market.  In effect, we view these investments=20
exactly like our successful controlled businesses - a permanent=20
part of Berkshire rather than merchandise to be disposed of once=20
Mr. Market offers us a sufficiently high price.  To that, I will=20
add one qualifier: These stocks are held by our insurance=20
companies and we would, if absolutely necessary, sell portions of=20
our holdings to pay extraordinary insurance losses.  We intend,=20
however, to manage our affairs so that sales are never required.=20

     A determination to have and to hold, which Charlie and I=20
share, obviously involves a mixture of personal and financial=20
considerations.  To some, our stand may seem highly eccentric.=20
(Charlie and I have long followed David Oglivy's advice: "Develop=20
your eccentricities while you are young.  That way, when you get=20
old, people won't think you're going ga-ga.") Certainly, in the=20
transaction-fixated Wall Street of recent years, our posture must=20
seem odd: To many in that arena, both companies and stocks are=20
seen only as raw material for trades.=20

     Our attitude, however, fits our personalities and the way we=20
want to live our lives.  Churchill once said, "You shape your=20
houses and then they shape you." We know the manner in which we=20
wish to be shaped.  For that reason, we would rather achieve a=20
return of X while associating with people whom we strongly like=20
and admire than realize 110% of X by exchanging these=20
relationships for uninteresting or unpleasant ones.  And we will=20
never find people we like and admire more than some of the main=20
participants at the three companies - our permanent holdings -=20
shown below:=20
