=20
     We really don't see many fundamental differences between the=20
purchase of a controlled business and the purchase of marketable=20
holdings such as these.  In each case we try to buy into=20
businesses with favorable long-term economics.  Our goal is to=20
find an outstanding business at a sensible price, not a mediocre=20
business at a bargain price.  Charlie and I have found that=20
making silk purses out of silk is the best that we can do; with=20
sow's ears, we fail.=20

     (It must be noted that your Chairman, always a quick study,=20
required only 20 years to recognize how important it was to buy=20
good businesses.  In the interim, I searched for "bargains" - and=20
had the misfortune to find some.  My punishment was an education=20
in the economics of short-line farm implement manufacturers,=20
third-place department stores, and New England textile=20
manufacturers.)=20
=20
     Of course, Charlie and I may misread the fundamental=20
economics of a business.  When that happens, we will encounter=20
problems whether that business is a wholly-owned subsidiary or a=20
marketable security, although it is usually far easier to exit=20
from the latter. (Indeed, businesses can be misread:  Witness the=20
European reporter who, after being sent to this country to=20
profile Andrew Carnegie, cabled his editor, "My God, you'll never=20
believe the sort of money there is in running libraries.")=20

     In making both control purchases and stock purchases, we try=20
to buy not only good businesses, but ones run by high-grade,=20
talented and likeable managers.  If we make a mistake about the=20
managers we link up with, the controlled company offers a certain=20
advantage because we have the power to effect change.  In=20
practice, however, this advantage is somewhat illusory:=20
Management changes, like marital changes, are painful, time-
consuming and chancy.  In any event, at our three marketable-but=20
permanent holdings, this point is moot:  With Tom Murphy and Dan=20
Burke at Cap Cities, Bill Snyder and Lou Simpson at GEICO, and=20
Kay Graham and Dick Simmons at The Washington Post, we simply=20
couldn't be in better hands.=20

     I would say that the controlled company offers two main=20
advantages.  First, when we control a company we get to allocate=20
capital, whereas we are likely to have little or nothing to say=20
about this process with marketable holdings.  This point can be=20
important because the heads of many companies are not skilled in=20
capital allocation.  Their inadequacy is not surprising.  Most=20
bosses rise to the top because they have excelled in an area such=20
as marketing, production, engineering, administration or,=20
sometimes, institutional politics.=20

     Once they become CEOs, they face new responsibilities.  They=20
now must make capital allocation decisions, a critical job that=20
they may have never tackled and that is not easily mastered.  To=20
stretch the point, it's as if the final step for a highly-
talented musician was not to perform at Carnegie Hall but,=20
instead, to be named Chairman of the Federal Reserve.=20

     The lack of skill that many CEOs have at capital allocation=20
is no small matter: After ten years on the job, a CEO whose=20
company annually retains earnings equal to 10% of net worth will=20
have been responsible for the deployment of more than 60% of all=20
the capital at work in the business.=20

     CEOs who recognize their lack of capital-allocation skills=20
(which not all do) will often try to compensate by turning to=20
their staffs, management consultants, or investment bankers. =20
Charlie and I have frequently observed the consequences of such=20
"help." On balance, we feel it is more likely to accentuate the=20
capital-allocation problem than to solve it.=20

     In the end, plenty of unintelligent capital allocation takes=20
place in corporate America. (That's why you hear so much about=20
"restructuring.") Berkshire, however, has been fortunate.  At the=20
companies that are our major non-controlled holdings, capital has=20
generally been well-deployed and, in some cases, brilliantly so.=20

     The second advantage of a controlled company over a=20
marketable security has to do with taxes.  Berkshire, as a=20
corporate holder, absorbs some significant tax costs through the=20
ownership of partial positions that we do not when our ownership=20
is 80%, or greater.  Such tax disadvantages have long been with=20
us, but changes in the tax code caused them to increase=20
significantly during the past year.  As a consequence, a given=20
business result can now deliver Berkshire financial results that=20
are as much as 50% better if they come from an 80%-or-greater=20
holding rather than from a lesser holding.=20

     The disadvantages of owning marketable securities are=20
sometimes offset by a huge advantage:  Occasionally the stock=20
market offers us the chance to buy non-controlling pieces of=20
extraordinary businesses at truly ridiculous prices -=20
dramatically below those commanded in negotiated transactions=20
that transfer control.  For example, we purchased our Washington=20
Post stock in 1973 at $5.63 per share, and per-share operating=20
earnings in 1987 after taxes were $10.30.  Similarly, Our GEICO=20
stock was purchased in 1976, 1979 and 1980 at an average of $6.67=20
per share, and after-tax operating earnings per share last year=20
were $9.01. In cases such as these, Mr. Market has proven to be a=20
mighty good friend.=20
=20
     An interesting accounting irony overlays a comparison of the=20
reported financial results of our controlled companies with those=20
of the permanent minority holdings listed above.  As you can see,=20
those three stocks have a market value of over $2 billion.  Yet=20
they produced only $11 million in reported after-tax earnings for=20
Berkshire in 1987.=20

     Accounting rules dictate that we take into income only the=20
dividends these companies pay us - which are little more than=20
nominal - rather than our share of their earnings, which in 1987=20
amounted to well over $100 million.  On the other hand,=20
accounting rules provide that the carrying value of these three=20
holdings - owned, as they are, by insurance companies - must be=20
recorded on our balance sheet at current market prices.  The=20
result: GAAP accounting lets us reflect in our net worth the up-
to-date underlying values of the businesses we partially own, but=20
does not let us reflect their underlying earnings in our income=20
account.=20

     In the case of our controlled companies, just the opposite=20
is true.  Here, we show full earnings in our income account but=20
never change asset values on our balance sheet, no matter how=20
much the value of a business might have increased since we=20
purchased it.=20

     Our mental approach to this accounting schizophrenia is to=20
ignore GAAP figures and to focus solely on the future earning=20
power of both our controlled and non-controlled businesses. =20
Using this approach, we establish our own ideas of business=20
value, keeping these independent from both the accounting values=20
shown on our books for controlled companies and the values placed=20
by a sometimes foolish market on our partially-owned companies. =20
It is this business value that we hope to increase at a=20
reasonable (or, preferably, unreasonable) rate in the years=20
ahead.=20

<b>Marketable Securities - Other</b>

     In addition to our three permanent common stock holdings, we=20
hold large quantities of marketable securities in our insurance=20
companies.  In selecting these, we can choose among five major=20
categories: (1) long-term common stock investments, (2) medium-
term fixed-income securities, (3) long-term fixed income=20
securities, (4) short-term cash equivalents, and (5) short-term=20
arbitrage commitments.=20

     We have no particular bias when it comes to choosing from=20
these categories.  We just continuously search among them for the=20
highest after-tax returns as measured by "mathematical=20
expectation," limiting ourselves always to investment=20
alternatives we think we understand.  Our criteria have nothing=20
to do with maximizing immediately reportable earnings; our goal,=20
rather, is to maximize eventual net worth.=20

   <b>o</b> Let's look first at common stocks.  During 1987 the stock=20
market was an area of much excitement but little net movement:=20
The Dow advanced 2.3% for the year.  You are aware, of course, of=20
the roller coaster ride that produced this minor change.  Mr.=20
Market was on a manic rampage until October and then experienced=20
a sudden, massive seizure.=20

     We have "professional" investors, those who manage many=20
billions, to thank for most of this turmoil.  Instead of focusing=20
on what businesses will do in the years ahead, many prestigious=20
money managers now focus on what they expect other money managers=20
to do in the days ahead.  For them, stocks are merely tokens in a=20
game, like the thimble and flatiron in Monopoly.=20
=20
     An extreme example of what their attitude leads to is=20
"portfolio insurance," a money-management strategy that many=20
leading investment advisors embraced in 1986-1987.  This strategy=20
- which is simply an exotically-labeled version of the small=20
speculator's stop-loss order dictates that ever increasing=20
portions of a stock portfolio, or their index-future equivalents,=20
be sold as prices decline.  The strategy says nothing else=20
matters: A downtick of a given magnitude automatically produces a=20
huge sell order.  According to the Brady Report, $60 billion to=20
$90 billion of equities were poised on this hair trigger in mid-
October of 1987.=20
=20
     If you've thought that investment advisors were hired to=20
invest, you may be bewildered by this technique.  After buying a=20
farm, would a rational owner next order his real estate agent to=20
start selling off pieces of it whenever a neighboring property=20
was sold at a lower price?  Or would you sell your house to=20
whatever bidder was available at 9:31 on some morning merely=20
because at 9:30 a similar house sold for less than it would have=20
brought on the previous day?=20
=20
     Moves like that, however, are what portfolio insurance tells=20
a pension fund or university to make when it owns a portion of=20
enterprises such as Ford or General Electric.  The less these=20
companies are being valued at, says this approach, the more=20
vigorously they should be sold.  As a "logical" corollary, the=20
approach commands the institutions to repurchase these companies=20
- <i>I'm not making this up</i> - once their prices have rebounded=20
significantly.  Considering that huge sums are controlled by=20
managers following such Alice-in-Wonderland practices, is it any=20
surprise that markets sometimes behave in aberrational fashion?=20

     Many commentators, however, have drawn an incorrect=20
conclusion upon observing recent events: They are fond of saying=20
that the small investor has no chance in a market now dominated=20
by the erratic behavior of the big boys.  This conclusion is dead=20
wrong: Such markets are ideal for any investor - small or large -=20
so long as he sticks to his investment knitting.  Volatility=20
caused by money managers who speculate irrationally with huge=20
sums will offer the true investor more chances to make=20
intelligent investment moves.  He can be hurt by such volatility=20
only if he is forced, by either financial or psychological=20
pressures, to sell at untoward times.=20

     At Berkshire, we have found little to do in stocks during=20
the past few years.  During the break in October, a few stocks=20
fell to prices that interested us, but we were unable to make=20
meaningful purchases before they rebounded.  At yearend 1987 we=20
had no major common stock investments (that is, over $50 million)=20
other than those we consider permanent or arbitrage holdings. =20
However, Mr. Market will offer us opportunities - you can be sure=20
of that - and, when he does, we will be willing and able to=20
participate.=20

   <b>o</b> In the meantime, our major parking place for money is=20
medium-term tax-exempt bonds, whose limited virtues I explained=20
in last year's annual report.  Though we both bought and sold=20
some of these bonds in 1987, our position changed little overall,=20
holding around $900 million.  A large portion of our bonds are=20
"grandfathered" under the Tax Reform Act of 1986, which means=20
they are fully tax-exempt.  Bonds currently purchased by=20
insurance companies are not.=20

     As an alternative to short-term cash equivalents, our=20
medium-term tax-exempts have - so far served us well.  They have=20
produced substantial extra income for us and are currently worth=20
a bit above our cost.  Regardless of their market price, we are=20
ready to dispose of our bonds whenever something better comes=20
along.=20

   <b>o</b> We continue to have an aversion to long-term bonds (and may=20
be making a serious mistake by not disliking medium-term bonds as=20
well).  Bonds are no better than the currency in which they are=20
denominated, and nothing we have seen in the past year - or past=20
decade - makes us enthusiastic about the long-term future of U.S.=20
currency.=20

     Our enormous trade deficit is causing various forms of=20
"claim checks" - U.S. government and corporate bonds, bank=20
deposits, etc. - to pile up in the hands of foreigners at a=20
distressing rate.  By default, our government has adopted an=20
approach to its finances patterned on that of Blanche DuBois, of=20
<i>A Streetcar Named Desire</i>, who said, "I have always depended on=20
the kindness of strangers." In this case, of course, the=20
"strangers" are relying on the integrity of our claim checks=20
although the plunging dollar has already made that proposition=20
expensive for them.=20

     The faith that foreigners are placing in us may be=20
misfounded.  When the claim checks outstanding grow sufficiently=20
numerous and when the issuing party can unilaterally determine=20
their purchasing power, the pressure on the issuer to dilute=20
their value by inflating the currency becomes almost=20
irresistible.  For the debtor government, the weapon of inflation=20
is the economic equivalent of the "H" bomb, and that is why very=20
few countries have been allowed to swamp the world with debt=20
denominated in their own currency.  Our past, relatively good=20
record for fiscal integrity has let us break this rule, but the=20
generosity accorded us is likely to intensify, rather than=20
relieve, the eventual pressure on us to inflate.  If we do=20
succumb to that pressure, it won't be just the foreign holders of=20
our claim checks who will suffer.  It will be all of us as well.=20

     Of course, the U.S. may take steps to stem our trade deficit=20
well before our position as a net debtor gets out of hand. (In=20
that respect, the falling dollar will help, though unfortunately=20
it will hurt in other ways.) Nevertheless, our government's=20
behavior in this test of its mettle is apt to be consistent with=20
its Scarlett O'Hara approach generally: "I'll think about it=20
tomorrow." And, almost inevitably, procrastination in facing up=20
to fiscal problems will have inflationary consequences.=20
=20
     Both the timing and the sweep of those consequences are=20
unpredictable.  But our inability to quantify or time the risk=20
does not mean we should ignore it.  While recognizing the=20
possibility that we may be wrong and that present interest rates=20
may adequately compensate for the inflationary risk, we retain a=20
general fear of long-term bonds.=20

     We are, however, willing to invest a moderate portion of our=20
funds in this category if we think we have a significant edge in=20
a specific security.  That willingness explains our holdings of=20
the Washington Public Power Supply Systems #1, #2 and #3 issues,=20
discussed in our 1984 report.  We added to our WPPSS position=20
during 1987.  At yearend, we had holdings with an amortized cost=20
of $240 million and a market value of $316 million, paying us=20
tax-exempt income of $34 million annually.=20

   <b>o</b> We continued to do well in arbitrage last year, though - or=20
perhaps because - we operated on a very limited scale.  We enter=20
into only a few arbitrage commitments each year and restrict=20
ourselves to large transactions that have been publicly=20
announced.  We do not participate in situations in which green-
mailers are attempting to put a target company "in play."=20

     We have practiced arbitrage on an opportunistic basis for=20
decades and, to date, our results have been quite good.  Though=20
we've never made an exact calculation, I believe that overall we=20
have averaged annual pre-tax returns of at least 25% from=20
arbitrage.  I'm quite sure we did better than that in 1987.  But=20
it should be emphasized that a really bad experience or two -=20
such as many arbitrage operations suffered in late 1987 - could=20
change the figures dramatically.=20

     Our only $50 million-plus arbitrage position at yearend 1987=20
was 1,096,200 shares of Allegis, with a cost of $76 million and a=20
market value of $78 million.=20

   <b>o</b> We had two other large holdings at yearend that do not fit=20
precisely into any of our five categories.  One was various=20
Texaco, Inc. bonds with short maturities, all purchased after=20
Texaco went into bankruptcy.  Were it not for the extraordinarily=20
strong capital position of our insurance companies, it would be=20
inappropriate for us to buy defaulted bonds.  At prices=20
prevailing after Texaco's bankruptcy filing, however, we regarded=20
these issues as by far the most attractive bond investment=20
available to us.=20

     On a worst-case basis with respect to the Pennzoil=20
litigation, we felt the bonds were likely to be worth about what=20
we paid for them.  Given a sensible settlement, which seemed=20
likely, we expected the bonds to be worth considerably more.  At=20
yearend our Texaco bonds were carried on our books at $104=20
million and had a market value of $119 million.=20

     By far our largest - and most publicized - investment in=20
1987 was a $700 million purchase of Salomon Inc 9% preferred=20
stock.  This preferred is convertible after three years into=20
Salomon common stock at $38 per share and, if not converted, will=20
be redeemed ratably over five years beginning October 31, 1995. =20
From most standpoints, this commitment fits into the medium-term=20
fixed-income securities category.  In addition, we have an=20
interesting conversion possibility.=20

     We, of course, have no special insights regarding the=20
direction or future profitability of investment banking.  By=20
their nature, the economics of this industry are far less=20
predictable than those of most other industries in which we have=20
major Commitments.  This unpredictability is one of the reasons=20
why our participation is in the form of a convertible preferred.=20

     What we do have a strong feeling about is the ability and=20
integrity of John Gutfreund, CEO of Salomon Inc.  Charlie and I=20
like, admire and trust John.  We first got to know him in 1976=20
when he played a key role in GEICO's escape from near-bankruptcy. =20
Several times since, we have seen John steer clients away from=20
transactions that would have been unwise, but that the client=20
clearly wanted to make - even though his advice provided no fee=20
to Salomon and acquiescence would have delivered a large fee. =20
Such service-above-self behavior is far from automatic in Wall=20
Street.=20

     For the reasons Charlie outlines on page 50, at yearend we=20
valued our Salomon investment at 98% of par, $14 million less=20
than our cost.  However, we believe there is a reasonable=20
likelihood that a leading, high-quality capital-raising and=20
market-making operation can average good returns on equity.  If=20
so, our conversion right will eventually prove to be valuable.=20
=20
     Two further comments about our investments in marketable=20
securities are appropriate.  First, we give you our usual=20
warning: Our holdings have changed since yearend and will=20
continue to do so without notice.=20

     The second comment is related: During 1987, as in some=20
earlier years, there was speculation in the press from time to=20
time about our purchase or sale of various securities.  These=20
stories were sometimes true, sometimes partially true, and other=20
times completely untrue.  Interestingly, there has been no=20
correlation between the size and prestige of the publication and=20
the accuracy of the report.  One dead-wrong rumor was given=20
considerable prominence by a major national magazine, and another=20
leading publication misled its readers by writing about an=20
arbitrage position as if it were a long-term investment=20
commitment. (In not naming names, I am observing the old warning=20
that it's not wise to pick fights with people who buy ink by the=20
barrel.)=20

     You should understand that we simply don't comment in any=20
way on rumors, whether they are true or false.  If we were to=20
deny the incorrect reports and refuse comment on the correct=20
ones, we would in effect be commenting on all.=20

     In a world in which big investment ideas are both limited=20
and valuable, we have no interest in telling potential=20
competitors what we are doing except to the extent required by=20
law.  We certainly don't expect others to tell us of their=20
investment ideas.  Nor would we expect a media company to=20
disclose news of acquisitions it was privately pursuing or a=20
journalist to tell his competitors about stories on which he is=20
working or sources he is using.=20

     I find it uncomfortable when friends or acquaintances=20
mention that they are buying X because it has been reported -=20
incorrectly - that Berkshire is a buyer.  However, I do not set=20
them straight.  If they want to participate in whatever Berkshire=20
actually is buying, they can always purchase Berkshire stock. =20
But perhaps that is too simple.  Usually, I suspect, they find it=20
more exciting to buy what is being talked about.  Whether that=20
strategy is more profitable is another question.=20

<b>Financing</b>
=20
     Shortly after yearend, Berkshire sold two issues of=20
debentures, totaling $250 million.  Both issues mature in 2018=20
and will be retired at an even pace through sinking fund=20
operations that begin in 1999.  Our overall interest cost, after=20
allowing for expenses of issuance, is slightly over 10%.  Salomon=20
was our investment banker, and its service was excellent.=20

     Despite our pessimistic views about inflation, our taste for=20
debt is quite limited.  To be sure, it is likely that Berkshire=20
could improve its return on equity by moving to a much higher,=20
though still conventional, debt-to-business-value ratio.  It's=20
even more likely that we could handle such a ratio, without=20
problems, under economic conditions far worse than any that have=20
prevailed since the early 1930s.=20

     But we do not wish it to be only likely that we can meet our=20
obligations; we wish that to be certain.  Thus we adhere to=20
policies - both in regard to debt and all other matters - that=20
will allow us to achieve acceptable long-term results under=20
extraordinarily adverse conditions, rather than optimal results=20
under a normal range of conditions.=20

     Good business or investment decisions will eventually=20
produce quite satisfactory economic results, with no aid from=20
leverage.  Therefore, it seems to us to be both foolish and=20
improper to risk what is important (including, necessarily, the=20
welfare of innocent bystanders such as policyholders and=20
employees) for some extra returns that are relatively=20
unimportant.  This view is not the product of either our=20
advancing age or prosperity: Our opinions about debt have=20
remained constant.=20

     However, we are not phobic about borrowing. (We're far from=20
believing that there is no fate worse than debt.) We are willing=20
to borrow an amount that we believe - on a worst-case basis -=20
will pose no threat to Berkshire's well-being.  Analyzing what=20
that amount might be, we can look to some important strengths=20
that would serve us well if major problems should engulf our=20
economy: Berkshire's earnings come from many diverse and well-
entrenched businesses; these businesses seldom require much=20
capital investment; what debt we have is structured well; and we=20
maintain major holdings of liquid assets.  Clearly, we could be=20
comfortable with a higher debt-to-business-value ratio than we=20
now have.=20

     One further aspect of our debt policy deserves comment:=20
Unlike many in the business world, we prefer to finance in=20
anticipation of need rather than in reaction to it.  A business=20
obtains the best financial results possible by managing both=20
sides of its balance sheet well.  This means obtaining the=20
highest-possible return on assets and the lowest-possible cost on=20
liabilities.  It would be convenient if opportunities for=20
intelligent action on both fronts coincided.  However, reason=20
tells us that just the opposite is likely to be the case: Tight=20
money conditions, which translate into high costs for=20
liabilities, will create the best opportunities for acquisitions,=20
and cheap money will cause assets to be bid to the sky.  Our=20
conclusion:  Action on the liability side should sometimes be=20
taken independent of any action on the asset side.=20

     Alas, what is "tight" and "cheap" money is far from clear at=20
any particular time.  We have no ability to forecast interest=20
rates and - maintaining our usual open-minded spirit - believe=20
that no one else can.  Therefore, we simply borrow when=20
conditions seem non-oppressive and hope that we will later find=20
intelligent expansion or acquisition opportunities, which - as we=20
have said - are most likely to pop up when conditions in the debt=20
market are clearly oppressive.  Our basic principle is that if=20
you want to shoot rare, fast-moving elephants, you should always=20
carry a loaded gun.=20

     Our fund-first, buy-or-expand-later policy almost always=20
penalizes near-term earnings.  For example, we are now earning=20
about 6 1/2% on the $250 million we recently raised at 10%, a=20
disparity that is currently costing us about $160,000 per week. =20
This negative spread is unimportant to us and will not cause us=20
to stretch for either acquisitions or higher-yielding short-term=20
instruments.  If we find the right sort of business elephant=20
within the next five years or so, the wait will have been=20
worthwhile.=20

<b>Miscellaneous </b>

     We hope to buy more businesses that are similar to the ones=20
we have, and we can use some help.  If you have a business that=20
fits the following criteria, call me or, preferably, write.=20

     Here's what we're looking for:=20

     (1) large purchases (at least $10 million of after-tax=20
         earnings),=20

     (2) demonstrated consistent earning power (future=20
         projections are of little interest to us, nor are=20
         "turnaround" situations),=20

     (3) businesses earning good returns on equity while=20
         employing little or no debt,=20

     (4) management in place (we can't supply it),=20

     (5) simple businesses (if there's lots of technology,=20
         we won't understand it),=20

     (6) an offering price (we don't want to waste our time=20
         or that of the seller by talking, even preliminarily,=20
         about a transaction when price is unknown).=20
=20
     We will not engage in unfriendly takeovers.  We can promise=20
complete confidentiality and a very fast answer - customarily=20
within five minutes - as to whether we're interested.  We prefer=20
to buy for cash, but will consider issuing stock when we receive=20
as much in intrinsic business value as we give.  We invite=20
potential sellers to check us out by contacting people with whom=20
we have done business in the past.  For the right business - and=20
the right people - we can provide a good home.=20
=20
     On the other hand, we frequently get approached about=20
acquisitions that don't come close to meeting our tests: new=20
ventures, turnarounds, auction-like sales, and the ever-popular=20
(among brokers) "I'm-sure-something-will-work-out-if-you-people-
get-to-know-each-other." None of these attracts us in the least.=20

     Besides being interested in the purchases of entire=20
businesses as described above, we are also interested in the=20
negotiated purchase of large, but not controlling, blocks of=20
stock comparable to those we hold in Cap Cities and Salomon.  We=20
have a special interest in purchasing convertible preferreds as a=20
long-term investment, as we did at Salomon.=20

