     And now a bit of <i>deja vu</i>.  Most of Berkshire's major=20
stockholders received their shares at yearend 1969 in a=20
liquidating distribution from Buffett Partnership, Ltd.  Some of=20
these former partners will remember that in 1962 I encountered=20
severe managerial problems at Dempster Mill Manufacturing Co., a=20
pump and farm implement manufacturing company that BPL=20
controlled.=20

     At that time, like now, I went to Charlie with problems that=20
were too tough for me to solve.  Charlie suggested the solution=20
might lie in a California friend of his, Harry Bottle, whose=20
special knack was never forgetting the fundamental.  I met Harry=20
in Los Angeles on April 17, 1962, and on April 23 he was in=20
Beatrice, Nebraska, running Dempster.  Our problems disappeared=20
almost immediately.  In my 1962 annual letter to partners, I=20
named Harry "Man of the Year."=20

     Fade to 24 years later: The scene is K &amp; W Products, a small=20
Berkshire subsidiary that produces automotive compounds.  For=20
years K &amp; W did well, but in 1985-86 it stumbled badly, as it=20
pursued the unattainable to the neglect of the achievable. =20
Charlie, who oversees K &amp; W, knew there was no need to consult=20
me.  Instead, he called Harry, now 68 years old, made him CEO,=20
and sat back to await the inevitable.  He didn't wait long.  In=20
1987 K &amp; W's profits set a record, up more than 300% from 1986. =20
And, as profits went up, capital employed went down: K &amp; W's=20
investment in accounts receivable and inventories has decreased=20
20%.=20

     If we run into another managerial problem ten or twenty=20
years down the road, you know whose phone will ring.=20

