     About 97.2% of all eligible shares participated in=20
Berkshire's 1987 shareholder-designated contributions program. =20
Contributions made through the program were $4.9 million, and=20
2,050 charities were recipients.=20

     A recent survey reported that about 50% of major American=20
companies match charitable contributions made by directors=20
(sometimes by a factor of three to one).  In effect, these=20
representatives of the owners direct funds to their favorite=20
charities, and never consult the owners as to their charitable=20
preferences. (I wonder how they would feel if the process were=20
reversed and shareholders could invade the directors' pockets for=20
charities favored by the shareholders.) When A takes money from B=20
to give to C and A is a legislator, the process is called=20
taxation.  But when A is an officer or director of a corporation,=20
it is called philanthropy.  We continue to believe that=20
contributions, aside from those with quite clear direct benefits=20
to the company, should reflect the charitable preferences of=20
owners rather than those of officers and directors.=20

     We urge new shareholders to read the description of our=20
shareholder-designated contributions program that appears on=20
pages 54 and 55.  If you wish to participate in future programs,=20
we strongly urge that you immediately make sure your shares are=20
registered in the name of the actual owner, not in "street" name=20
or nominee name.  Shares not so registered on September 30, l988=20
will be ineligible for the 1988 program.=20
